  "items": [
    {
      "id": "p0042-b001",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        42.0,
        57.0,
        439.0,
        106.0
      ],
      "markdown": "the state has so high persistence it is known (Papaspiliopoulos *et al.*, 2003) that the centred Gibbs sampler (for $T=\\infty$) has better mixing. The robustness of PG sampling is again very promising. Note that the SPF and PMMH methods have worse performance for small values of the ratio, which is due to the deterioration of the bootstrap filter with decreasing observation error. This deterioration appears to have no effect on PG sampling in this simple setting.",
      "source_class": "text",
      "join_previous": "space"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b002",
      "kind": "heading",
      "bbox": [
        42.0,
        116.0,
        404.0,
        135.0
      ],
      "markdown": "### Krzysztof Łatuszyński (University of Toronto) and Omiros Papaspiliopoulos (Universitat Pompeu Fabra, Barcelona)",
      "source_class": "text"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b003",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        42.0,
        136.0,
        439.0,
        185.0
      ],
      "markdown": "We congraulate the authors for a beautiful paper. A fundamental idea is the interplay between unbiased estimation (by means of importance sampling in this paper) and exact simulation. We show how unbiased estimation relates to exact simulation of events of unknown probability $s\\in[0,1]$. Details, proofs and an application to the celebrated Bernoulli factory problem (Nacu and Peres, 2005) can be found in Łatuszński *et al.* (2009).",
      "source_class": "text"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b004",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        42.0,
        184.0,
        439.0,
        205.0
      ],
      "markdown": "We wish to simulate the binary random variable $C_s$ such that $P[C_s=1]=s$. If $\\hat S$ is a realizable unbiased estimator of $s$ taking values in $[0,1]$, we use the following algorithm 1.",
      "source_class": "text"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b005",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        51.0,
        209.0,
        163.0,
        219.0
      ],
      "markdown": "*Step 1*: simulate $G_0\\sim U(0,1)$.",
      "source_class": "text"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b006",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        51.0,
        217.0,
        115.0,
        229.0
      ],
      "markdown": "*Step 2*: obtain $\\hat S$.",
      "source_class": "text"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b007",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        51.0,
        227.0,
        238.0,
        239.0
      ],
      "markdown": "*Step 3*: if $G_0\\leqslant\\hat S$ set $C_s:=1$; otherwise set $C_s:=0$.",
      "source_class": "text"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b008",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        51.0,
        239.0,
        120.0,
        248.0
      ],
      "markdown": "*Step 4*: output $C_s$.",
      "source_class": "text"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b009",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        42.0,
        253.0,
        439.0,
        272.0
      ],
      "markdown": "If $l_1,l_2,\\ldots$ and $u_1,u_2,\\ldots$ are sequences of lower and upper bounds converging monotonically to $s$ then we can resort to the following algorithm 2.",
      "source_class": "text"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b010",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        51.0,
        276.0,
        198.0,
        285.0
      ],
      "markdown": "*Step 1*: simulate $G_0\\sim U(0,1)$; set $n=1$.",
      "source_class": "text"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b011",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        51.0,
        286.0,
        152.0,
        295.0
      ],
      "markdown": "*Step 2*: compute $l_n$ and $u_n$.",
      "source_class": "text"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b012",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        51.0,
        296.0,
        157.0,
        305.0
      ],
      "markdown": "*Step 3*: if $G_0\\leqslant l_n$ set $C_s:=1$.",
      "source_class": "text"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b013",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        51.0,
        306.0,
        160.0,
        315.0
      ],
      "markdown": "*Step 4*: if $G_0>u_n$ set $C_s:=0$.",
      "source_class": "text"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b014",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        51.0,
        316.0,
        249.0,
        325.0
      ],
      "markdown": "*Step 5*: if $l_n<G_0\\leqslant u_n$ set $n:=n+1$ and go to step 2.",
      "source_class": "text"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b015",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        51.0,
        326.0,
        120.0,
        335.0
      ],
      "markdown": "*Step 6*: output $C_s$.",
      "source_class": "text"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b016",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        42.0,
        343.0,
        439.0,
        362.0
      ],
      "markdown": "We can combine these ideas to have unbiased estimators $L_n$ and $U_n$ of $l_n$ and $u_n$. The estimators live on the same probability space and have the following properties:",
      "source_class": "text"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b017",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        149.0,
        364.0,
        444.0,
        384.0
      ],
      "markdown": "$$\\mathbb{P}(L_n\\leqslant U_n)=1\\qquad\\text{for every }n=1,2,\\ldots;\\tag{46}$$",
      "source_class": "formula"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b018",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        100.0,
        380.0,
        444.0,
        400.0
      ],
      "markdown": "$$\\mathbb{P}(L_n\\in[0,1]=1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\mathbb{P}(U_n\\in[0,1])=1\\qquad\\text{for every }n=1,2,\\ldots;\\tag{47}$$",
      "source_class": "formula"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b019",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        163.0,
        398.0,
        444.0,
        417.0
      ],
      "markdown": "$$\\mathbb{E}(L_n)=l_n\\nearrow s\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\mathbb{E}(U_n)=u_n\\searrow s;\\tag{48}$$",
      "source_class": "formula"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b020",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        152.0,
        415.0,
        444.0,
        434.0
      ],
      "markdown": "$$\\mathbb{P}(L_{n-1}\\leqslant L_n)=1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\mathbb{P}(U_{n-1}\\geqslant U_n)=1.\\tag{49}$$",
      "source_class": "formula"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b021",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        42.0,
        435.0,
        56.0,
        442.0
      ],
      "markdown": "Let",
      "source_class": "section-header"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b022",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        157.0,
        444.0,
        324.0,
        490.0
      ],
      "markdown": "$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{F}_0&=\\{\\emptyset,\\Omega\\},\\\\\\mathcal{F}_n&=\\sigma\\{L_n,U_n\\},\\\\\\mathcal{F}_{k,n}&=\\sigma\\{\\mathcal{F}_k,\\mathcal{F}_{k+1},\\ldots,\\mathcal{F}_n\\}\\qquad k\\leqslant n.\\end{aligned}$$",
      "source_class": "formula"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b023",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        42.0,
        492.0,
        282.0,
        502.0
      ],
      "markdown": "Under these assumptions we can use the following algorithm 3.",
      "source_class": "text"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b024",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        51.0,
        510.0,
        199.0,
        519.0
      ],
      "markdown": "*Step 1*: simulate $G_0\\sim U(0,1)$; set $n=1$.",
      "source_class": "text"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b025",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        51.0,
        520.0,
        197.0,
        529.0
      ],
      "markdown": "*Step 2*: obtain $L_n$ and $U_n$ given $\\mathcal{F}_{0,n-1}$.",
      "source_class": "text"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b026",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        51.0,
        529.0,
        161.0,
        539.0
      ],
      "markdown": "*Step 3*: if $G_0\\leqslant L_n$ set $C_s:=1$.",
      "source_class": "text"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b027",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        51.0,
        539.0,
        161.0,
        549.0
      ],
      "markdown": "*Step 4*: if $G_0>U_n$ set $C_s:=0$.",
      "source_class": "text"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b028",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        51,
        549,
        254,
        559
      ],
      "markdown": "*Step 5*: if $L_n<G_0\\leqslant U_n$ set $n:=n+1$ and go to step 2.",
      "source_class": "text"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-r001",
      "kind": "text",
      "markdown": "*Step 6*: output $C_s$.",
      "bbox": [
        51,
        559,
        254,
        569
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b029",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        42.0,
        577.0,
        439.0,
        597.0
      ],
      "markdown": "The final step is to weaken condition (49) and to let $L_n$ be a reverse time supermartingale and $U_n$ a reverse time submartingale with respect to $\\mathcal{F}_{n,\\infty}$. Precisely, assume that for every $n=1,2,\\ldots$ we have",
      "source_class": "text"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b030",
      "kind": "text",
      "bbox": [
        129,
        598,
        444,
        616
      ],
      "markdown": "$$\\mathbb{E}(L_{n-1}|\\mathcal{F}_{n,\\infty})=\\mathbb{E}(L_{n-1}|\\mathcal{F}_n)\\leqslant L_n\\qquad\\text{almost surely},\\tag{50}$$",
      "source_class": "formula"
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-r002",
      "kind": "text",
      "markdown": "$$\\mathbb{E}(U_{n-1}|\\mathcal{F}_{n,\\infty})=\\mathbb{E}(U_{n-1}|\\mathcal{F}_n)\\geqslant U_n\\qquad\\text{almost surely}.\\tag{51}$$",
      "bbox": [
        129,
        616,
        444,
        634
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p0042-b000",
      "kind": "omit",
      "bbox": [
        42.0,
        39.0,
        317.0,
        48.0
      ],
      "markdown": "310 _Discussion on the Paper by Andrieu, Doucet and Holenstein_",
      "source_class": "page-header",
      "reason": "Running header with page number (page furniture)."
    }
  ]
